&lt;h1 class="relative group">The Story of Nyquist, Shannon, and the Science of Sampling
&lt;div id="the-story-of-nyquist-shannon-and-the-science-of-sampling" class="anchor">&lt;/div>
&lt;span
class="absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 ltr:-left-6 rtl:-right-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100">
&lt;a class="group-hover:text-primary-300 dark:group-hover:text-neutral-700"
style="text-decoration-line: none !important;" href="#the-story-of-nyquist-shannon-and-the-science-of-sampling" aria-label="Anchor">#&lt;/a>
&lt;/span>
&lt;/h1>
&lt;p>In the early days of the 20th century, the world was just beginning to hum with the sounds of electric voices. Telephones crackled across cities, Morse code blinked from station to station, and engineers at &lt;strong>Bell Telephone Laboratories&lt;/strong> worked tirelessly to harness the invisible — the flow of information through wires.&lt;/p></description></item><item><title>BitNet b1.58-2B4T: Revolutionary Binary Neural Network for Efficient AI</title><link>https://dasarpai.github.io/dsblog/bitnet-b1-58-2b4t-for-efficient-ai-processing/</link><pubDate>Mon, 21 Apr 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><author>hari@dasarpai.com (Dr. Hari Thapliyaal)</author><guid>https://dasarpai.github.io/dsblog/bitnet-b1-58-2b4t-for-efficient-ai-processing/</guid><description>&lt;p>
